+{
+  "title": "Harbourline Freight — Jira Data Center to Cloud Migration Readiness Assessment",
+  "content": "## 1. Executive Summary\n\nHarbourline Freight runs **Jira Data Center 9.12.x on a single node with PostgreSQL**, with Confluence out of scope for this assessment. The site is on the short side of a published, hard clock: Atlassian announced the **end of life for Data Center on 28 March 2029**, and the last date on which an existing customer can buy subscriptions, Marketplace apps, or user expansions is **30 March 2028**.\n\nWe modelled the user estate (430 accounts) and project estate (15 projects, 173,879 issues in total) and produced a per-user identity plan and a per-project migration plan. The headline findings are:\n\n- **309 of 430 users** can be migrated directly; **55** are case-only duplicate email pairs that should be merged before cutover; **36** should be skipped (service accounts or inactive past the 24-month threshold); **30** need human review (no email address).\n- **9 projects** can migrate in the first wave; **1** (FRT, 70,412 issues) should be a separate wave; **4** are candidates for archiving rather than migrating; **1** (EDI) needs its lead reviewed because the lead is a service account.\n- **Four project leads** would have been dropped by the inactivity rule alone — including two who have not logged in for five years or more — and would have been left owning live projects. They have all been exempted.\n\n> **Important:** the user and project datasets used here are a **synthetic model** of the estate, built from the profile described at the 2026-09-27 kickoff call. The actual user export was under NDA and was not available to us. All counts in this report are the shape of the estate, not its exact contents. The scripts that produced these plans are included and will be re-run against the live export before anything is actioned.\n\n## 2. Scope\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Product | Jira Data Center 9.12.x, single node, PostgreSQL |\n| Confluence | Out of scope for this assessment |\n| Users | 430 accounts (modelled) |\n| Projects | 15 projects, 173,879 issues in total |\n| Decision inputs | 24-month inactivity cutoff (per Aoife Brennan), project leads never dropped |\n| Deliverables | This report; re-runnable assessment scripts |\n\nThe assessment covers the identity-resolution and project-waving phases of the migration. It does not cover field/automation/attachment mapping, Cloud edition sizing beyond flagging the drivers, or the Confluence estate.\n\n## 3. User Findings\n\nOf 430 modelled accounts, the identity plan produced the following decisions:\n\n| Decision | Count | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Migrate | 309 | Active, unique, has an email address |\n| Merge | 55 | Case-only duplicate email — fold into the most recently active twin |\n| Skip | 36 | Service account, or inactive for more than 24 months |\n| Review | 30 | No email address — needs a human decision |\n\nThe dominant data-quality finding is **55 case-only duplicate email pairs** — two accounts for one person that differ only in letter casing. This is the classic consequence of the AD synchronisation being switched off in 2023. Our plan merges each pair into the most recently active account.\n\nAtlassian's own migration tooling will refuse to migrate duplicate emails, and its \"merge duplicates\" option selects the surviving account **at random** — so leaving these to the migration is leaving the choice to chance. Merging first, on paper, lets the most recently active person keep their history.\n\nFour project leads are exempted from the skip rule despite being inactive:\n\n| Lead | Project(s) | Last login | Without the lead rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| aoconnor | CUS, PLAN | 2021-11-13 | Skipped — inactive 1,780 days |\n| dbyrne | FLT | 2021-07-31 | Skipped — inactive 1,885 days |\n| jfarrell | SALES | 2020-06-01 | Skipped — inactive 2,310 days |\n| svc-edi | EDI | 2019-03-21 | Skipped — service account and inactive |\n\nThe case to put in front of management is the last two: a project lead not seen for six years still holds a live project's ownership. That is either a strong case that the rule is protecting real accountability, or a sign that a stale ownership record needs fixing — either way, it is a conversation, not a cleanup.\n\n## 4. Project Findings\n\nThe project plan produced the following decisions across the 15 projects:\n\n| Decision | Count | Projects |\n|---|---|---|\n| Migrate — wave 1 | 9 | WHS, CUS, FIN, HR, FLT, PORT, SAFE, ITSD, SALES |\n| Separate wave | 1 | FRT (70,412 issues — 40% of the site's total) |\n| Archive candidate | 4 | OLDWMS, MIG19, TRN, PLAN |\n| Lead review | 1 | EDI (lead is a service account) |\n\nFRT is worth naming on its own. At 70,412 issues it is 40% of the site's 173,879 issues, and it is the number Marek gave us by hand at the kickoff, which is what makes it a real finding rather than an estimate. Migrating it in the same wave as the other nine would put 40% of the site on one cutover.\n\nThe four archive candidates are all untouched for two years or more, and are a combined 3,728 issues — 2% of the site. Archiving them reduces the migration's surface and lets the client focus on the 11 live projects.\n\n## 5. Data Center End of Life\n\nThe following dates come from Atlassian's own announcement and should be the frame in which the client reads every other number in this report.\n\n| Date | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **30 March 2026** | End of sales to new customers |\n| **30 March 2028** | End of sales to existing customers — no new subscriptions, apps, or user expansions |\n| **28 March 2029** | End of life — all Data Center products and Marketplace apps go read-only |\n\nA practical consequence worth stating plainly: the client has **less than two years** to migrate, and effectively **less than 12 months** in which to buy anything, once the 30 March 2028 purchase cutoff has passed.\n\nSources:\n\n- https://www.atlassian.com/licensing/data-center-end-of-life\n- https://support.atlassian.com/migration/docs/update-or-install-the-jira-cloud-migration-assistant/\n\n## 6. Risks\n\n| Risk | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 30 no-email accounts cannot be matched at cutover | Work orphaned, licences wasted on ghost accounts | Resolve during the review phase before wave 1 | Marek |\n| 55 case-only duplicates left to the migration | Atlassian merges at random — someone keeps a stranger's history | Pre-migration merge per the identity plan | Mihai |\n| FRT (70k issues) shares a wave with the rest | 40% of the site on one cutover — long outage window, hard rollback | Separate wave, own cutover window | Aoife |\n| Four leads dropped by the inactivity rule | Ownership vacated on live projects, including svc-edi on EDI | Lead override is in the plan; confirm ownership with each lead | Aoife |\n| 3,728 issues in 4 archive candidates migrate anyway | Licences and clutter carried over needlessly | Archive before wave 1; confirm no live reporting points at them | Marek |\n| No-Cloud edition decision pending | Standard vs Premium affects cost and capacity headroom | Run the sizing script against the live export before the next call | Mihai |\n\n## 7. Next Steps\n\n| When | What | Owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| By 2026-10-02 call | Resolve the 30 no-email accounts — each needs a decision (merge, skip, or keep) | Marek |\n| By 2026-10-02 call | Confirm the lead ownership for aoconnor, dbyrne, jfarrell, and svc-edi | Aoife |\n| By 2026-10-02 call | Present edition sizing (Standard vs Premium) — needs live export to be exact | Mihai |\n| Before wave 1 | Run the identity and project plans against the real data export | Mihai |\n| Before wave 1 | Execute the 55 case-only merges per the plan | Mihai |\n| Before wave 1 | Archive the 4 archive candidates | Marek |\n\n---\n*Prepared 2026-09-29. All figures are from the modelled dataset described in the Executive Summary.*\n",
+  "stylePreset": "professional",
+  "category": "business",
+  "docType": "formal",
+  "tags": [
+    "harbourline",
+    "migration",
+    "readiness",
+    "jira"
+  ],
+  "clientHint": "agent"
+}
